WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
The first film followed DNA changes through one expanding lineage.

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.500
How can mixed DNA reads reveal which tumor lineage carries a change?

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.120
Start with that same clay-colored family tree.

4
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.640
An early clay marker appears near its trunk.

5
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.720
Every later branch inherits it.

6
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.090
Later, one branch gains a second, square marker.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.090
Only that branch carries it, so it is a subclone.

8
00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.190
The trunk marker is clonal because every tumor branch carries it.

9
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.090
The pattern records ancestry, even when the sample mixes all branches together.

10
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.293
Now place a matched-normal sample beside the tumor.

11
00:00:46.473 --> 00:00:49.193
It comes from the same person, often from blood.

12
00:00:50.453 --> 00:00:53.673
A variant found only in tumor is usually somatic.

13
00:00:54.893 --> 00:00:59.533
A variant found in both samples is usually germline, most often inherited.

14
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:02.153
The word usually matters.

15
00:01:03.283 --> 00:01:06.643
Here is a gold variant in both tumor and matched normal.

16
00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:12.063
Predict: did it most likely enter this person's cells before the tumor lineage

17
00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:15.063
began, or after one tumor branch formed?

18
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.780
Most likely, it was present before the tumor lineage began.

19
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.960
So it is usually germline, whether inherited or newly formed in an egg or sperm.

20
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.220
The matched normal gives the timing clue.

21
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.240
Tumor data alone cannot reliably separate that history from a somatic change.

22
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:46.883
Variant allele fraction, or VAF, is the fraction of reads carrying a variant.

23
00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:50.723
A clonal variant can still sit below one half.

24
00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:54.103
Normal cells dilute the tumor reads.

25
00:01:55.503 --> 00:02:01.383
Copy gains, losses, and loss of heterozygosity also change the number of variant and

26
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.383
nonvariant copies.

27
00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:07.537
Purity is the fraction of cells in the sample that are tumor cells.

28
00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:15.097
For a heterozygous clonal variant in a two-copy region, expected VAF is purity times

29
00:02:15.097 --> 00:02:20.317
one half. The shortcut also assumes normal cells lack the variant.

30
00:02:21.497 --> 00:02:26.697
It fails after copy changes or loss of the other allele, or when only a subclone

31
00:02:26.697 --> 00:02:27.697
carries it.

32
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.230
So read the evidence in order.

33
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.170
Compare tumor with matched normal.

34
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.330
Ask which lineages carry the variant.

35
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.890
Then count mutant copies against all copies.

36
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.370
Purity, copy number, and read depth limit the inference.

37
00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.190
The result is a family-tree estimate, not a perfect replay.

38
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.640
A high or low fraction is not a verdict by itself.

39
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.260
Read it with the matched normal and the sample's copy structure.

40
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.060
Different branch histories can produce similar fractions, so the supporting clues

41
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.480
matter. That uncertainty belongs in the interpretation.

42
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.560
Together, those clues connect mixed sequencing reads back to the expanding cell

43
00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.240
lineages that produced them.
